
\markboth{PREFACE}{PREFACE}
\pdfbookmark{Preface}{preface}
\chapter*{Preface}

\vspace{.2in}

\begin{quote}
``{\em Some half dozen persons have written technically on combinatory
logic, and most of these, including ourselves, have published
something erroneous.  Since some of our fellow sinners are among the
most careful and competent logicians on the contemporary scene, we
regard this as evidence that the subject is refractory.  Thus fullness
of exposition is necessary for accuracy; and excessive condensation
would be false economy here, even more than it is ordinarily.}''
\begin{flushright}
Haskell B.~Curry and Robert Feys \\
in the Preface to {\em Combinatory Logic} \cite{curry&feys:book}, May 31, 1956
\end{flushright}
\end{quote}

\vspace{.2in}

\noindent
In September of 1987 a meeting was held at the conference on
Functional Programming
Languages and Computer Architecture (FPCA~'87) in
Portland, Oregon, to discuss an unfortunate situation
in the functional programming community: there had come into being
more than a dozen non-strict, purely functional programming languages,
all similar in expressive power and semantic underpinnings.  There
was a strong consensus at this meeting that more widespread use of
this class of functional languages\index{functional language} was
being hampered by the lack of a common language.  It was decided
that a committee should be formed to design such a language, providing
faster communication of new ideas, a stable foundation for real
applications development, and a vehicle through which others
would be encouraged to use functional languages.  This
document describes the result of that (and subsequent)
committee's efforts: a purely
functional programming language called \Haskell{},
\index{Haskell@@\Haskell{}}
named after the logician Haskell B.~Curry\index{Curry, Haskell B.}
whose work provides the logical basis for much of ours.

\section*{Goals}

The committee's primary goal was to design a language that
satisfied these constraints:
\begin{enumerate}
\item It should be suitable for teaching, research, and applications,
      including building large systems.
\item It should be completely described via the publication of a formal
      syntax and semantics.
\item It should be freely available.  Anyone should be permitted to
      implement the language and distribute it to whomever they please.
\item It should be based on ideas that enjoy a wide consensus.
\item It should reduce unnecessary diversity in functional
      programming languages.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{\Haskell{} 2024: language and libraries}

The committee intended that \Haskell{} would serve as a basis for
future research in language design, and hoped that extensions or
variants of the language would appear, incorporating experimental
features.

\Haskell{} has indeed evolved continuously since its original publication.
By the middle of 1997, there had been five versions of the language design
(from \Haskell{} 1.0 -- 1.4).  At the 1997 \Haskell{} Workshop
in Amsterdam, it was decided that a stable variant of \Haskell{} was
needed; this became ``\Haskell{} 98'' and was published in February
1999.  The fixing of minor bugs led to the \emph{Revised} \Haskell{} 98 Report
in 2002.

\begin{haskellprime}
At the 2005 \Haskell{} Workshop, the consensus was that so many extensions
to the official language were widely used (and supported by multiple
implementations), that it was worthwhile to define another iteration of
the language standard, essentially to codify (and legitimise) the status
quo.

The \Haskell{} Prime effort was thus conceived as a relatively
conservative extension of \Haskell{} 98, taking on board new features
only where they were well understood and widely agreed upon.  It too
was intended to be a ``stable'' language, yet reflecting the
considerable progress in research on language design in recent years.

After several years exploring the design space, it was decided that a
single monolithic revision of the language was too large a task, and
the best way to make progress was to evolve the language in small
incremental steps, each revision integrating only a small number of
well-understood extensions and changes.  \Haskell{} 2010 is the first
revision to be created in this way, and new revisions are expected
once per year.
\end{haskellprime}

\begin{haskelltf}
The Haskell 2010 Language Report has been, and continues to be, a valued
reference for users of Haskell. However, much has happened since it was first
published. There has been further progress in research on language design and
Haskell, and Haskell modules, have continued to evolve.

Despite that progress, the hope that the report would be the first of annual
incremental revisions to the language has not been realised.

Although not the only Haskell compiler, the Glasgow Haskell Compiler (GHC) is,
by some margin, the most popular one today. It has used the @LANGUAGE@ pragma
to introduce many extensions to the Haskell 2010 language. With the release of
GHC 9.2.1 in October 2021, it introduced the language feature @GHC2021@
(a {\em language edition}) to specify a set of enabled extensions. GHC 9.10.1,
released in May 2024, introduced the further language edition @GHC2024@.

The Haskell 2024 Language Report aims to modify the Haskell 2010 Language Report
as if the extensions in @GHC2024@ were part of the language and @forall@ were a
reserved identifier. In doing so, it draws upon each extension's approved
proposal and the documentation in, or referred to, by the relevant part of the
GHC User's Guide.

The Prelude and other Haskell modules have also changed since the Haskell 2010
Language Report. The Haskell 2024 Language Report aims to reflect such changes
only to the extent strictly necessary to be consistent with the extensions in
@GHC2024@ and by reference to the boot packages of GHC 9.10.3 as published on
Hackage. In that regard:
\begin{itemize}
\item The Prelude exports classes @Semigroup@, @Monoid@, @Applicative@,
      @MonadFail@, @Foldable@ and @Traversable@.
\item @Applicative@ is a superclass of @Monad@.
\item Functions @null@, @length@, @notElem@, @and@, @or@, @any@, @all@,
      @concat@, @concatMap@, @mapM_@ and @sequence_@ (exported by the Prelude)
      are more general than applying to lists.
\item @(<$) :: a -> f b -> fa@ is a method of the class @Functor@.
\item @fail :: String -> m a@ is not a method of the class @Monad@ and is a
      method of the class @MonadFail@.
\end{itemize}
\end{haskelltf}

\section*{Extensions to \Haskell{} 98}

\begin{haskellprime}
The most significant language changes in \Haskell{} 2010 relative to
\Haskell{} 98 are listed here.

New language features:
\begin{itemize}
\item A Foreign Function Interface (FFI).
\item Hierarchical module names, e.g. @Data.Bool@.
\item Pattern guards.
\end{itemize}

Removed language features:
\begin{itemize}
\item The $(n+k)$ pattern syntax.
\end{itemize}

\end{haskellprime}

\section*{Extensions to \Haskell{} 2010}

\begin{haskelltf}

@forall@ is a reserved identifier (see Sections~\ref{ids},
\ref{lexical-syntax}).

@GHC2024@ removes contexts from the declarations of algebraic datatypes (see
Section~\ref{user-defined-datatypes}) and datatype renamings (see
Section~\ref{datatype-renaming}), which was a part of Haskell 2010.

@GHC2024@ replaces CUSKS ({\em complete user-supplied kind signatures}) with
standalone kind signatures. [The report is not yet modified for this matter.]

@GHC2024@ also enables the following new features (the classification is that of
the GHC User's Guide):

\begin{tabular}{llp{4cm}}
Classification & Feature & [Report modified?] \\
\hline
Syntax & Empty case alternatives & Sections~\ref{case}, \ref{bnf} \\
Syntax & Lambda-case &  Sections~\ref{case}, \ref{layout}, \ref{bnf} \\
Syntax & Postfix operators & Section~\ref{sections} \\
Syntax & Tuple sections & Section~\ref{sections}, \ref{bnf} \\
Import/export & Writing qualified in postpositive position & Section~\ref{import}, \ref{bnf} \\
Literals & Binary integer literals & Sections~\ref{lexemes}, \ref{lexemes-numeric}, \ref{lexical-syntax} \\
Literals & Hexadecimal floating point literals & Sections~\ref{lexemes-numeric}, \ref{lexical-syntax} \\
Literals & Numeric underscores & Sections~\ref{lexemes-numeric}, \ref{lexical-syntax} \\
Types	& Datatype promotion & No \\
Types	& Existentially quantified data constructors (explicit forall) & No \\
Types	& GADTs (GADT Syntax, Mono local binds) & No \\
Types & Allow kind polymorphic types (kind signatures) & No \\
Types & Rank-n types (explicit forall) & No \\
Types	& Role annotations & No \\
Types & Visible type application & No \\
Types & Type operators (explicit namespaces) & No \\
Records & Disambiguate record fields & No \\
Records & Named field puns & No \\
Records & Named wild cards & No \\
Deriving & Derive @Data@ and @Typeable@ instances & No \\
Deriving & Derive @Generic@ instances & Sections~\ref{derived-decls},
\ref{derived-generic} \\
Deriving & Derive @Lift@ instances & No \\
Deriving & Derive @Functor@ instances & Sections~\ref{derived-decls},
\ref{functor-class}, \ref{stdprelude}, \ref{derived-functor}\\
Deriving & Derive @Foldable@ instances & Sections~\ref{derived-decls},
\ref{semigroup-class}, \ref{monoid-class}, \ref{foldable-class},
\ref{stdprelude}, \ref{preludelist}, \ref{derived-foldable}\\
Deriving & Derive @Traversable@ instances & Sections~\ref{derived-decls},
\ref{applicative-class}, \ref{monad-class}, \ref{traversable-class},
\ref{stdprelude}, \ref{preludelist}, \ref{derived-traversable}\\
Deriving & Deriving strategies & Sections~\ref{declarations},
\ref{datatype-decls}, \ref{datatype-renaming}, \ref{derived-decls}, \ref{bnf},
\ref{deriving-strategies}, \ref{stock-deriving} \\
Deriving & Derive instances for empty data types & Sections~\ref{stock-deriving},
\ref{derived-eq-ord}, \ref{derived-functor}, \ref{derived-foldable},
\ref{derived-traversable} \\
Deriving & Generalised derived instances for newtypes & Sections~\ref{derived-appendix},
\ref{newtype-deriving} \\
Deriving & Stand-alone deriving declarations & Section~\ref{declarations},
\ref{derived-decls}, \ref{bnf}, \ref{derived-appendix} \\
Class/instance & Flexible instances (type synonym instances) & No \\
Class/instance & Type signatures in instance declarations & No \\
Class/instance & Multi-parameter type classes (constrained class methods) & No \\
Constraints & The @Constraint@ kind & No \\
Constraints & Loosening restractions on class contexts & No \\
Type signatures &	Lexically scoped type variables (explicit forall) & No \\
Bang patterns & Bang patterns & No \\
\end{tabular}
\end{haskelltf}

\section*{\Haskell{} Resources}
\label{sect:resources}

The \Haskell{} web site \url{http://haskell.org} gives access to many useful resources, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item Online versions of the language and library definitions.
\item Tutorial material on \Haskell{}.
\item Details of the \Haskell{} mailing list.
\item Implementations of \Haskell{}.
\item Contributed \Haskell{} tools and libraries.
\item Applications of \Haskell{}.
\item User-contributed wiki pages.
\item News and events in the \Haskell{} community.
\end{itemize}
You are welcome to comment on, suggest improvements to, and criticise
the language or its presentation in the report, via the \Haskell{}
mailing list, or the \Haskell{} wiki.

\section*{Building the language}

\Haskell{} was created, and continues to be sustained, by an active
community of researchers and application programmers.  Those who
served on the Language and Library committees, in particular, devoted
a huge amount of time and energy to the language.  Here they are, with
their affiliation(s) for the relevant period:

